\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces The seven worlds, one frame each: three backgrounds (black, gray noise redrawn every frame, gray noise frozen for the episode) crossed with the hidden wind, which is not rendered, so \texttt  {still} and \texttt  {wind} frames come from the same distribution. The gate world is shown before and after the reveal, solid at $t=26$ and with the gap at $t=30$.}}{15}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:worlds}{{2}{15}{The seven worlds, one frame each: three backgrounds (black, gray noise redrawn every frame, gray noise frozen for the episode) crossed with the hidden wind, which is not rendered, so \texttt {still} and \texttt {wind} frames come from the same distribution. The gate world is shown before and after the reveal, solid at $t=26$ and with the gap at $t=30$}{figure.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Why the gate world is a decision problem, three true episodes under the same wall, which enters solid at $t=22$ and has its gap drawn in at $t=29$ when it reaches $x=39$. Top: an agent holding its start column at $x=20$ has five steps left at the reveal and needs five moves to reach either gap, so it is caught and dies at $t=34$. Middle and bottom: an agent that has retreated further left reacts after the reveal and lives, for a gap on top and for a gap at the bottom. Nothing in the frame before $t=29$ distinguishes the two cases, so the information has to be waited for.}}{15}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:gate}{{3}{15}{Why the gate world is a decision problem, three true episodes under the same wall, which enters solid at $t=22$ and has its gap drawn in at $t=29$ when it reaches $x=39$. Top: an agent holding its start column at $x=20$ has five steps left at the reveal and needs five moves to reach either gap, so it is caught and dies at $t=34$. Middle and bottom: an agent that has retreated further left reacts after the reveal and lives, for a gap on top and for a gap at the bottom. Nothing in the frame before $t=29$ distinguishes the two cases, so the information has to be waited for}{figure.3}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces What each model learned. For the JEPA arms, the final prediction term of Equation\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {eq:lewm} and the held-out correlation $r$ between the off-budget reward head on the frozen latent and the true reward; for the flow model, gFVD, action faithfulness, and the area under the curve of its termination head on real and on imagined rollouts. A low prediction loss beside a reward correlation near zero is the pattern Proposition\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {prop:representation} constructs: the two \emph  {noise per episode} rows have the lowest losses in the table and the worst read-outs. On the four noise worlds the flow model's gFVD is dominated by the unpredictable texture, so action faithfulness is the informative rollout number there. The flow model's reward correlation is not reported: the only non-zero reward is the terminal one and our evaluation windows contain no episode end, which leaves the correlation undefined. Its termination read-outs are unaffected.}}{15}{table.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:models}{{3}{15}{What each model learned. For the JEPA arms, the final prediction term of Equation~\ref {eq:lewm} and the held-out correlation $r$ between the off-budget reward head on the frozen latent and the true reward; for the flow model, gFVD, action faithfulness, and the area under the curve of its termination head on real and on imagined rollouts. A low prediction loss beside a reward correlation near zero is the pattern Proposition~\ref {prop:representation} constructs: the two \emph {noise per episode} rows have the lowest losses in the table and the worst read-outs. On the four noise worlds the flow model's gFVD is dominated by the unpredictable texture, so action faithfulness is the informative rollout number there. The flow model's reward correlation is not reported: the only non-zero reward is the terminal one and our evaluation windows contain no episode end, which leaves the correlation undefined. Its termination read-outs are unaffected}{table.3}{}}
