\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Survival by controller in the six cube worlds, the same numbers as Table\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {tab:cube}. The two JEPAs fail in complementary worlds: the class-token arm holds up under a per-episode wallpaper and collapses under per-frame noise, the grid arm does the reverse and collapses whenever the wind is on. PPO inside the flow model clears every world.}}{17}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:cube-results}{{4}{17}{Survival by controller in the six cube worlds, the same numbers as Table~\ref {tab:cube}. The two JEPAs fail in complementary worlds: the class-token arm holds up under a per-episode wallpaper and collapses under per-frame noise, the grid arm does the reverse and collapses whenever the wind is on. PPO inside the flow model clears every world}{figure.4}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces What the models believe about the wall in the gate world, measured on the decoded rollout as the fraction of the true wall's pixel mass that is present, over the same contexts as Figure\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {fig:predictions}. Decoding the latent of the \emph  {real} future separates the representation from the predictor: the grid JEPA keeps the wall almost perfectly when told what actually happened and loses it under its own mean rollout. This is a pixel measurement in line with Lemma\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {lem:mean}, not an instance of it, since the decoder is nonlinear. The sampling-predictor row uses the same encoder and heads and differs only in drawing $z_{t+1}$ instead of returning its mean. The last column asks whether the final frame commits to a side.}}{17}{table.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:wallmass}{{4}{17}{What the models believe about the wall in the gate world, measured on the decoded rollout as the fraction of the true wall's pixel mass that is present, over the same contexts as Figure~\ref {fig:predictions}. Decoding the latent of the \emph {real} future separates the representation from the predictor: the grid JEPA keeps the wall almost perfectly when told what actually happened and loses it under its own mean rollout. This is a pixel measurement in line with Lemma~\ref {lem:mean}, not an instance of it, since the decoder is nonlinear. The sampling-predictor row uses the same encoder and heads and differs only in drawing $z_{t+1}$ instead of returning its mean. The last column asks whether the final frame commits to a side}{table.4}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces One gate context, wall still solid, and what each model believes will happen. Rows $1$ and $2$ are the two real futures. Rows $3$ and $5$ decode the encoder's latent of the \emph  {true} future and so measure the representation alone: the grid JEPA holds the wall and the gap sharply, the class token does not hold them at all. Rows $4$ and $6$ decode each model's own mean rollout and so measure the predictor: the grid JEPA's wall is absent rather than blurred across both positions. Rows $7$ to $9$ are three samples from the flow model, which keep the wall and disagree about the side. The two failures appear in separate rows, which is the point of the row layout.}}{18}{figure.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:predictions}{{5}{18}{One gate context, wall still solid, and what each model believes will happen. Rows $1$ and $2$ are the two real futures. Rows $3$ and $5$ decode the encoder's latent of the \emph {true} future and so measure the representation alone: the grid JEPA holds the wall and the gap sharply, the class token does not hold them at all. Rows $4$ and $6$ decode each model's own mean rollout and so measure the predictor: the grid JEPA's wall is absent rather than blurred across both positions. Rows $7$ to $9$ are three samples from the flow model, which keep the wall and disagree about the side. The two failures appear in separate rows, which is the point of the row layout}{figure.5}{}}
